6.1  Network layers: OSI and TCP/IP

TL;DR
  - A network is built in layers, each using the one below: link (Ethernet,
    Wi-Fi), network (IP), transport (TCP, UDP), application (HTTP, DNS, SSH).
  - The OSI model names seven layers, the Internet uses four, and people still
    say "layer 2" for Ethernet and "layer 7" for HTTP.
  - When something fails, check from the bottom up:

      ip -br link                     # 2: is the link up?
      ip -br addr; ip route           # 3: an address, and a way out?
      ping -c 3 1.1.1.1               # 3: does IP reach the Internet?
      dig example.com                 # 7: do names resolve?
      curl -I https://example.com     # 4 to 7: TCP, TLS, HTTP

On your machine
  L2 link   wlp0s20f3 up · neighbour 192.168.1.1 is f4:92:bf:0e:21:7a on
            wlp0s20f3
  L3 IP     docker0 172.17.0.1/16, wlp0s20f3 192.168.1.23/24, wlp0s20f3
            fe80::8ec6:81ff:fe2a:107f/64 · gateway 192.168.1.1 · forwards
            packets for others
  L4 TCP    12 connections open now; 30,120 started and 410 accepted since boot
            · 1,840 of 3.1 million segments resent
  L4 UDP    95,000 datagrams in, 61,000 out, since boot
  L7 DNS    127.0.0.53

Pitfalls
  - ping 1.1.1.1 works but ping example.com doesn't: it's DNS, not the network.
  - ping uses ICMP, which some networks block: a failed ping doesn't prove a
    host is down.
  - OSI layers 5 and 6 (session, presentation) have no clear place of their own
    on the Internet; TLS is usually put between 4 and 7.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 6.1 --why

